  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing Offline Requirements**\n\nI\'m currently breaking down the user\'s offline functionality requirements. It appears they need a completely offline application, and I\'m noting the need for an input section for devices with quantities, like an LED light, fan, TV, etc. I\'m focusing on the architecture for the input system, ensuring it aligns with the offline-first design.\n\n\n**Defining Load Profiles**\n\nI\'ve expanded the device list to include common household appliances (pump, microwave, laptop). Now, I\'m focusing on user-defined backup durations to suggest appropriate grid options: On-grid, Off-grid, or Hybrid, explaining the pros, cons, and necessary components for each. My next step is to address calculating peak load, surge wattage, and current, and tailor setup guidelines. I\'m also planning to incorporate LFP cell options with capacities from 15Ah to 314Ah, specifying type (Cylindrical or Prismatic) and cycle life. I\'ll design a custom cell data class, and then create a cycle life calculation system.\n\n\n**Modeling Cell Options**\n\nI\'m now focusing on specific LFP cell models, creating an enumeration with common cylindrical and prismatic options, from 6Ah to 314Ah, and defining their base cycle lives. The user will be able to select from these models. I\'m ensuring the selection will affect capacity and chemistry properties. I\'m also ensuring the user can configure series and parallel cells to determine voltage and scale capacity. Finally, the user can set Depth of Discharge (DoD) from 10% to 100%. The cycle life will automatically scale based on the DoD.\n\n\n**Enumerating Appliances**\n\nI\'m now detailing household appliances, including wattage and surge multipliers. I\'m building a system to capture quantities for each, calculating total running watts and required backup hours. I\'ll automatically determine total required energy and display the results clearly for the user. I\'ll define appliances from LED lights to air conditioners and more.\n\n\n"
