    thinking: "**Analyzing Battery ViewModel**\n\nI\'m now diving into `BatteryViewModel.kt`. My focus is elegance: how can I incorporate all these intricate requirements seamlessly? First up, I\'m thinking about defining a data class specifically for LFP cell types. I want to start by crafting a solution for this particular aspect.\n\n\n**Defining Cell Choices**\n\nI\'ve sketched out an `LfpCellChoice` data class with model name, capacity, type, and base cycle life. I also have an enum for `CellType`. Now, I\'m working on a pre-defined list of LFP cell choices representing common real-world cells, but I\'ve realized cycle life must adjust based on DoD.\n\n\n**Calculating Cycle Life**\n\nI\'ve crafted a function, `calculateCycleLife`, which now accurately estimates cycle life relative to depth of discharge. It provides cycle counts relative to the DoD percent, from 20% to 100%. I\'ve considered that cycle life changes non-linearly, and have added a sliding scale, with a good range. The logic is now encapsulated, for easy integration.\n\n\n**Formulating Appliance Inputs**\n\nI\'m now formulating the `ApplianceInput` class to handle household devices. It includes fields for ID, names in Bangla and English, wattage, surge multiplier, and icon. I am now working on a list of typical household appliances, like LED bulbs and ceiling fans. I\'m also ensuring the model responds dynamically to the cell selected and the configured DoD settings.\n\n\n**Defining Appliance Parameters**\n\nI\'ve finalized the `ApplianceInput` parameters, including nominal wattage and surge multipliers for devices like refrigerators and air conditioners. Now, I\'m integrating a list of common household appliances, complete with power consumption and surge data. I\'m focusing on user customization of appliance quantities and backup hours. I\'ll calculate total active load, total surge load, starting surge current, and target backup hours.\n\n\n**Determining Backup Hour Logic**\n\nI\'m now implementing the global backup hours slider. My focus is on calculating the energy requirement efficiently, specifically: (Total Active Continuous Load * Global Target Backup Hours). Then, I\'ll calculate the required battery pack capacity, taking into account pack voltage and depth of discharge. Finally, I will display whether the currently selected battery is sufficient, underpowered, or oversized. I\'m keen on making this easy to understand.\n\n\n**Defining Grid Options**\n\nI\'m now detailing the grid options. My plan is to let the user select between \"Off-Grid\" and \"On-Grid\". I\'m outlining their respective pros, cons, and requirements. The goal is to provide a clear explanation to the user. I\'m focusing on simplicity and clarity, so I\'m also including Bangla translations for each of these options.\n\n\n**Defining Grid Options**\n\nI\'ve fleshed out the \"Hybrid\" grid option, detailing its advantages and disadvantages. This includes blackout protection, flexibility, and grid feed-in. I also included its more complex design. I\'m aiming for clarity in describing these complex concepts. The final logic will analyze active watts, surge watts, and starting surge amperes to customize system recommendations.\n\n\n"
    modified_response: "I will now write the updated `BatteryViewModel.kt` to encompass LFP cell choices (with capacity ranges from 15Ah to 314Ah, and auto-computed cycle lives based on form factors and depth of discharge), household appliance lists with peak inductive surge multipliers, and full support for On-grid / Off-grid / Hybrid solar system recommendation calculations."
