  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Exploring Persistent Storage**\n\nI\'m now investigating Android\'s SharedPreferences as a potential solution for automatically saving a user\'s device selections and configuration results. I believe this will ensure the app retains their choices even after they close and reopen it. This method seems promising.\n\n\n**Refining Configuration Persistence**\n\nI\'ve decided SharedPreferences is perfect and lightweight for saving active state details. I\'ll save quantities of selected appliances, the selected cell choice ID, series cells count, parallel cells count, DoD percent, target backup hours, and selected grid type. I will implement auto-saving in `BatteryViewModel` using `saveCurrentStateToPrefs` and `loadStateFromPrefs` methods, ensuring automatic persistence without a \"Save\" button. I will save them to SharedPreferences when they change, and load them on init. This approach complements saving named configurations in the Room database. I\'m focusing on the variables in `BatteryViewModel.kt` now.\n\n\n**Implementing Reactive State Persistence**\n\nI\'m now contemplating how to reactively save `depthOfDischargePercent`, `targetBackupHours`, and `selectedGridType` to SharedPreferences. I\'m exploring the potential of utilizing Kotlin\'s `mutableStateOf` coupled with custom getters/setters or helper functions that trigger persistence upon state modifications. I\'m also considering creating simple setter functions or a general `saveState` helper function. This approach aims to streamline the saving process, so that state changes automatically trigger the save. I want to ensure the state updates persist efficiently, even when triggered from other functions or settings changes.\n\n\n**Streamlining State Updates**\n\nI\'m now determining the most efficient way to handle state updates. I can either create specific update functions within `BatteryViewModel` like `updateSeriesCells` and `updateParallelCells` to encompass the save to SharedPreferences logic, or implement reactive wrappers. The current goal is to ensure that modifications to `seriesCells`, `parallelCells`, `depthOfDischargePercent`, `targetBackupHours`, and `selectedGridType` in `BatteryCalculatorScreen.kt` trigger automatic saving. I\'m leaning toward wrapping each modifiable parameter into setter and getter methods within the ViewModel.\n\n\n**Defining Persistence Implementation**\n\nI\'m now adding the specific update functions within `BatteryViewModel.kt`, namely `updateSeriesCells`, `updateParallelCells`, `updateDepthOfDischarge`, `updateTargetBackupHours`, and `updateSelectedGridType`, incorporating SharedPreferences saving. I will also add the corresponding load mechanism. I\'m leveraging `getApplication<Application>()` to access SharedPreferences via the application context. I\'ve defined the keys for each parameter: `selected_cell_id`, `series_cells`, `parallel_cells`, `dod_percent`, `target_backup_hours`, and `selected_grid_type`. I\'ll also add saving of appliance IDs and quantities with the format `appliance_qty_$id`. I\'m starting the implementation of `savePreferences()` and aiming for a robust, error-safe load and save mechanism for SharedPreferences.\n\n\n**Finalizing SharedPreferences Implementation**\n\nI\'ve just completed the `savePreferences()` function, incorporating all parameters for saving to SharedPreferences: the `selectedCellChoice` ID, `seriesCells`, `parallelCells`, `depthOfDischargePercent`, `targetBackupHours`, `selectedGridType`, and appliance quantities.  I\'ve added the `loadPreferences()` function, which restores saved configurations on app start, also. I am using `apply()` for asynchronous saving.\n\n\n**Refining Setter Implementation**\n\nI\'m now implementing the setter methods within the `BatteryViewModel.kt` file. I\'ve finished the `loadPreferences` method, ensuring data retrieval on app start. The logic includes default values for first-time use, and now I\'m working to make the setters trigger an update to SharedPreferences.\n\n\n**Integrating Save Operations**\n\nI\'m now incorporating calls to `savePreferences()` within `applySVoltPreset`, `applyCellChoice`, `incrementAppliance`, `decrementAppliance`, and `loadConfig` to guarantee that all relevant state changes trigger a save. I\'ve updated the update functions to call `savePreferences` when they are called.\n\n\n**Verifying Screen Integration**\n\nI\'m now integrating the setter methods into `BatteryCalculatorScreen.kt` to ensure all state changes trigger save operations. I am verifying that the screen updates the `selectedCellChoice` correctly, so I can call the setter and trigger saving. I will also check the other values I have saved, namely `incrementAppliance` and `decrementAppliance`, as they are the primary functions to trigger saves, as of now.\n\n\n\n"
