  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Designing the Implementation**\n\nI\'m now focusing on integrating the unit conversion utility. I plan to append `LfpProToolsTab` and its helper functions to `BatteryCalculatorScreen.kt` after line 1996. My immediate priority is carefully designing the new composables to ensure a user-friendly implementation of the requested unit toggling functionality.\n\n\n**Defining Capacity Measurements**\n\nI\'ve been examining the array of battery capacity measurements to be supported: Ah, Wh, kWh, Joules, MJ, and BTU. My approach centers on a user-friendly selector. I\'m currently leaning towards a clear visual design. My goal is to use a dropdown (like `ExposedDropdownMenuBox`) or perhaps scrollable choice chips, or even a dialog to keep it accessible. The \"Ah\" selection is causing me to design a voltage input.\n\n\n**Crafting the UI Component**\n\nI\'m now focused on constructing a custom, inline dropdown UI element. The goal is a component that presents options elegantly without visual errors. Localization is a priority. I\'ve drafted a precise conversion function that handles all capacity units to Wh as a base unit before converting, and all\'s well, apart from needing the user voltage if the Ah unit is involved. I\'ll take care of this next.\n\n\n**Implementing Voltage Input**\n\nI\'m now integrating voltage input for the Ampere-hour calculations. It\'ll be a dedicated slider/input, shown only when \"Ah\" is selected in the unit dropdown. I am also working on a visually appealing LFP energy calculator screen.\n\nI\'m currently designing an \"LFP Energy & Voltage Calculator\" card within the tab. I\'m focusing on user-friendliness and realism, and incorporating input fields for Cell Capacity (with presets!), Nominal Voltage, Series (S), and Parallel (P) configurations. The aim is to create a dynamic, real-time display of the calculations.\n\n\n**Visualizing the Discharge Curve**\n\nI\'m now implementing the LFP voltage discharge curve visualization using an interactive Canvas chart. I\'ve modeled a realistic LiFePO4 cell discharge curve at a C/5 rate, and defined specific voltage points at each state of charge to drive the visualization\'s accuracy and realism. The next step is to make this display interactive, so the user can easily see these calculations at a glance.\n\n\n**Modeling the Discharge Curve**\n\nNow I\'m focusing on the mathematical representation of the LFP cell discharge curve. I\'ve defined the voltage points at varying states of charge, from 0% to 100%. I\'ve written a function to calculate the LFP cell voltage given any SoC percentage, using a piecewise linear approximation for an accurate and realistic visualization.\n\n\n**Visualizing Total Pack Voltage**\n\nI\'m now integrating the series cell count to show total pack voltage in real-time. This includes a smooth LFP voltage curve with both knees and a 3.2V plateau. I\'m focusing on making the chart respond dynamically to the user\'s settings. I\'ll display both Cell and Total Pack Voltage on the chart based on series cells. The x-axis will represent SoC (100% to 0%), the y-axis (2.0V to 3.8V or equivalent pack voltage).\n\n\n**Drawing the Visual Grid**\n\nI\'m now adding a visual grid to the chart, using dotted lines for SoC percentages and voltages. Then, I\'ll add the lithium-ion glowing gradient brush for the curve, followed by touch/drag interaction.\n\nI\'m planning the touch interaction: mapping the X coordinate to SoC to calculate the voltage and display an interactive crosshair with a tooltip displaying the SoC, cell voltage, and pack voltage.\nI\'m now figuring out the best way to handle touch/drag gestures. I can detect tap and drag gestures using `Modifier.pointerInput(Unit)`.\n\n\n**Refining User Interaction**\n\nI\'m now integrating pointer input for both tap and drag gestures for the interactive chart, using a `pointerInput` block and `detectDragGestures` to track and respond to user interactions. I\'ll translate the user\'s touch position to a state-of-charge (SoC) value, calculate the corresponding voltage, and display an interactive crosshair with a tooltip displaying the SoC, cell voltage, and pack voltage. I\'m also planning the custom Canvas drawing, starting with finding dimensions and setting up padding for the axes and labels.\n\n\n**Mapping the Canvas Coordinates**\n\nI\'ve been designing the coordinate system for the interactive chart, and it\'s elegant. The calculations for mapping SoC and cell voltage to the X and Y coordinates respectively are working smoothly. Now I\'ll create the axes and grid lines and add labels to the plot. Then I\'ll focus on drawing the discharge curve itself. The discharge curve visualization is coming into focus.\n\n\n**Adding the Final Touches**\n\nI\'m now implementing the interactive crosshair and tooltip for the discharge curve visualization. My plan is to add a vertical line and tooltip at the touch position. I\'m choosing an overlay info panel or a dedicated card below the chart to display the active tooltip values (SoC, Cell V, Pack V). I\'m planning to use a beautiful card, highly readable and localized, ensuring a clean and accessible user interface.\n\n\n"
    modified_response: "I will view lines 110 to 145 of `/app/src/main/java/com/example/ui/BatteryCalculatorScreen.kt` to make sure we have the exact lines for modifying the tabs and the state variables in the parent `BatteryCalculatorScreen` Composable."
